{
  "AC": "- imgcomp/ is a utils Python package: create_pipenv.sh venv, make test runs .venv/bin/pytest (fast, headless).\n- Object ABC: center-based local pixel coords; sample(x, y) -> 32-bit straight RGBA; hit(x, y) -> bool. A Circle hits iff distance from the object center to the pixel is <= radius. sample and hit are independent (a hit region may include fully transparent samples); tests do not enforce coupling.\n- Loaded-image hit is the axis-aligned rectangle of the bitmap, including transparent texels.\n- Compositor ABC: viewport is a stated W x H in pixels; render(root) -> Surface; pick(vx, vy) -> topmost hit object in depth-first paint order (or None). Surface ABC is width/height plus pixel read/write and must not require numpy, so Apple-native, Cython, and C-FFI backends can implement it later.\n- Integer buffer pixel (i, j) samples/picks at continuous coord (i+0.5, j+0.5). Viewport (0,0) is top-left, +y down. An untranslated object's center sits at viewport center (W/2, H/2).\n- NaiveCompositor (pure Python) composites the scene tree depth-first with src-over (straight alpha) into a CPU Surface. Later siblings paint on top of earlier ones. Wrappers pull-sample (transform the query coord, then ask the child) rather than rasterize-then-filter.\n- Primitives: loaded image; computed Circle. Wrappers: Translate (place child center in parent space), Rotate about local origin, Group, and a simple ColorMod on straight RGBA.\n- Live preview shows the buffer in a window and may downsample; the scene viewport stays full resolution. Offline path writes a full-res PNG with no window (the markup export path).\n- pick() selects the topmost hit. Wrappers percolate the event to the contained object. Handlers receive local center-based coords (object origin 0,0), not viewport pixels. Objects may supply on_touch / on_drag / on_scroll. No bubbling past the picked object's wrapper chain to siblings.\n- Out of scope this ticket: Apple Metal/CoreGraphics, Cython, and C-FFI compositor implementations. Only NaiveCompositor ships; the ABCs are the seam.\n- Guard tests: sample/hit contracts (including Circle), DFS overlap order, image AABB hit, offline PNG round-trip, pick() dispatch with local coords. make test does not require a display.",
  "ActualSummary": "Landed imgcomp/ on master in one commit (sha:2ec6e29): Object/Surface/Compositor ABCs, NaiveCompositor with DFS src-over, Circle and ImageObject, Translate/Rotate/Group/ColorMod wrappers, offline PNG export, Tk live preview, and pick/dispatch events. 20 headless pytest tests pass including end-to-end markup export and touch dispatch.",
  "BaseSha": "14a757dfb3d87665c557218c1b8d3e234903a28b",
  "Body": {
    "objid": "0002",
    "raw": "A small structured 2D graphics library whose job is to mark up images: a tree of objects, composited depth-first into a viewport of stated pixel size, then shown live (optionally downsampled) and exported at full resolution.\n\nWHY. The motivating product is overlaying computed and loaded graphics on photographs (and similar bitmaps) without a full GUI toolkit or a game engine. Python should be enough to sketch scenes and interactions. Later we may accelerate the inner loop with Cython, C-FFI, or Apple-native drawing; that is why the compositor is an ABC, not a pile of numpy loops with no seam.\n\nMODEL (SDF-like, not a retained-mode widget kit):\n\n- An Object maps local coordinates to 32-bit straight RGBA (sample) and to a boolean hit (hit). Local coordinates are center-based and in pixels: for a Circle, hit is Euclidean distance from the object center to the queried pixel.\n- sample and hit are independent APIs. A weak, unenforced design assumption is that the hit region is a superset of where sample has visible color (hit may include alpha-0 pixels, e.g. a Circle whose fill is smaller than its hit radius). Tests do not require sample alpha > 0 iff hit.\n- Base objects: a loaded image, and computed objects (Circle first). Image hit is the axis-aligned rectangle of the bitmap, including transparent texels, so a photo is easy to grab. Image sample still returns the texel (possibly fully transparent) at that local point.\n- Wrappers are Objects that wrap other Objects: Translate (place the child's center in parent space), Rotate about the local origin, Group (ordered children), and a simple ColorMod (multiply RGB/alpha, straight). Composition is the tree, not a separate blend-graph language.\n- A Compositor has a viewport size in pixels and paints the root Object. NaiveCompositor walks the tree depth-first, src-over (straight alpha), later siblings on top. Pull-sampling: the compositor (or a wrapper) asks \"what color is at this parent-space pixel?\" and the child converts to local space. That matches the SDF analogy and keeps rotation correct without a raster-then-rotate pass.\n- Objects may be reactive: optional on_touch, on_drag, on_scroll. pick() selects the topmost hit in DFS paint order. Wrappers percolate the event to the contained object. Handlers receive local center-based coords (origin at the object's center), not viewport pixel indices. No bubbling to sibling objects. Desktop live window: mouse down = touch, drag with button held, wheel = scroll. Display-downsample mapping (window pixel -> full-res viewport pixel, then into local space) is required for live events.\n\nCOORDINATES (ratified for image markup):\n\n- Viewport buffer: (0,0) is the top-left pixel, +x right, +y down. Size is W x H as stated, not inferred from the window.\n- Integer pixel (i, j) is sampled and picked at continuous coordinate (i+0.5, j+0.5), the center of that pixel. sample/hit take floats so rotation does not snap.\n- Object local space: origin at the object center, same +y down, units = pixels.\n- An untranslated object's center sits at the viewport center (W/2, H/2). Translate then moves that center in parent space.\n\nBACKEND SEAM (do not implement accelerated backends on this ticket):\n\n- Compositor and Surface are ABCs. NaiveCompositor + a CPU Surface (bytes or array.array, not a numpy requirement) is the only concrete compositor.\n- Surface must not require numpy so a later Apple backend can wrap CGImage/IOSurface, and a C-FFI backend can wrap a C buffer, without pretending those are ndarrays.\n- Object.sample / Object.hit remain the source of truth for tests and the naive path. A future GPU compositor may bake the tree; it still has to match sample() on a test grid.\n- Planned later subclasses (names illustrative): CythonCompositor, CFFICompositor, AppleCompositor. Out of scope here.\n\nLIVE vs OFFLINE:\n\n- Live: a window showing the current buffer. Preview may downsample (e.g. fit longest side to ~800px) so a 12MP markup stays interactive. The scene viewport stays full res; downsampling is display-only.\n- Offline: render at full viewport resolution to PNG with Pillow; no window, so it runs headless. This is the markup export path.\n- Live toolkit: Tk plus Pillow (stdlib Tk, already-needed image I/O). Swap the window later for an NSView without changing Object/Compositor.\n\nBLEND: src-over on straight (non-premultiplied) RGBA only. Extra blend modes are not this ticket. ColorMod is defined on straight channels.\n\nDECISIONS RATIFIED IN GROOMING (2026-08-24):\n\n  - IMAGE HIT: AABB of the bitmap, including transparent texels.\n  - SAMPLE vs HIT: independent. Weak assumption hit-region >= visible-sample region; not enforced.\n  - DEFAULT PLACEMENT: untranslated object center at viewport center (W/2, H/2).\n  - PIXEL SAMPLING: buffer (i, j) <-> continuous (i+0.5, j+0.5).\n  - ALPHA: straight RGBA; src-over and ColorMod documented for that; Pillow import/export without premul conversion in v1.\n  - EVENTS: topmost pick; wrappers percolate to the contained object; handler coords are local 0,0-center; no sibling bubbling.\n\nTRACKER: sequential WorkItems on this one ticket, no subtodos. Single new package, one actor, ordered chunks (scaffold -> ABC exemplar -> naive paint -> primitives -> wrappers -> live -> offline -> events). HICAP owns the ABC + guard tests; the rest is MIDCAP. Do not start Apple/Cython/C-FFI implementation from an ungroomed follow-on.\n\nPATH: imgcomp/ at the utils repo root (same layout as shadup/: requirements.txt, Makefile test, .venv via ./create_pipenv.sh imgcomp, .www/blurb.md for make index)."
  },
  "Branch": "04278e67-2d-compositor-image-mar",
  "Id": "04278e67217e109cf213cf308358d868b779cd6b97d9a22a00cd6cfc76f74fc8",
  "LongSummary": {
    "objid": "0006",
    "raw": "New utils package imgcomp/: a structured 2D scene graph for marking up images. Objects are SDF-like maps from center-based local pixel coordinates to 32-bit straight RGBA (sample) and to a boolean hit test. sample and hit are independent: a Circle may hit a larger disk than it paints. A loaded image hits its full axis-aligned bitmap rectangle, including transparent texels. Integer buffer pixel (i, j) is queried at (i+0.5, j+0.5). An untranslated object's center sits at the viewport center (W/2, H/2). The tree is composited depth-first, src-over, into a viewport of stated pixel size.\n\nThe compositor is an ABC so a naive pure-Python CPU backend can ship first and later give way to Cython, C-FFI, or Apple-native (CGImage, IOSurface, Metal) implementations without rewriting the scene objects. Surface is also an ABC: width, height, pixel access, no numpy requirement. NaiveCompositor pull-samples the tree (wrappers transform query coords, then ask the child). Live preview may downsample the buffer in a Tk window while the scene stays full resolution; offline export writes a full-res PNG with Pillow and needs no display. Translate, Rotate, Group, and ColorMod wrap children. pick() takes the topmost hit; wrappers percolate events to the contained object in local center-based coordinates.\n\nAccelerated backends are out of scope for this ticket. Work is sequential WorkItems on one branch, no subtodos: scaffold, HICAP ABC exemplar, then MIDCAP naive paint, primitives, wrappers, live preview, offline PNG, and reactive dispatch."
  },
